 [(2,'PCA supplies cluster membership, whereas the later clustering step merely changes the coordinate axes.'),(4,'Both steps necessarily assign the same groups because neither requires ground-truth class labels.'),(1,'PCA supplies a reduced-coordinate representation; the separate clustering step supplies group membership.'),(3,'PCA requires class labels, whereas only the later clustering step can operate on unlabeled observations.')],[1],
 {1:'PCA is a linear dimensionality-reduction method. Group assignment is the stated purpose of the separate clustering stage; membership in an unsupervised-learning category does not make the operations identical.',2:'This exchanges the roles: a PCA projection is not itself a set of cluster labels.',3:'PCA does not require ground-truth class labels for its standard unsupervised dimensionality-reduction role.',4:'The lack of labels is shared context, not proof of identical outputs or grouping.'},
